  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: 'Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first { to the last }: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own. The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.' Reply one is {\"a\":1} {\"text\": \"{\"b\":2}\", \"extra\": 3}. Reply two is {\"a\":2} {\"text\": \"{\"b\":2}\", \"extra\": 3}. Under the rule, reply one's only top-level balanced object is {\"a\":1}, so the check must return {\"a\":1}; reply two's only top-level balanced object is {\"a\":2}, so the check must return {\"a\":2}. I expect the check to return the same nested object {\"b\":2} for both inputs, so the execution should be unchanged. The mechanism is that raw_decode fails on the invalid outer object {\"text\": \"{\"b\":2}\", \"extra\": 3}, leaving the nested '{' inside its string to be scanned as a top-level candidate, and the reversed pool returns that nested object. The observation that would show this conjecture false is if the check returns {\"a\":1} for reply one and {\"a\":2} for reply two, or raises on either input.",
